<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Champasak</h1><p class="st-meta">1713 CE – 1946 CE · capital Champasak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern Lao kingdom on the Mekong around Wat Phu, founded in 1713, whose princes later led the southern Lao nobility in modern Laos.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1713 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soysisamouth">Soysisamouth</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sourigna-vongsa">Sourigna Vongsa</a>, was installed as king at Champasak in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> with the support of the monk Phra Khu Phonsamek, separating the south from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-vientiane">Kingdom of Vientiane</a>. The region contained the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phu">Wat Phu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Champasak ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> plains of the south and upland areas with Mon-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> peoples, trading forest products, cardamom and slaves. From 1778 it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Siam, which later governed much of its territory directly. The kingdom's capital moved several times along the river near Bassac.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Franco-Siamese treaties of 1893 and 1904, Champasak passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-of-laos">French Laos</a>, the 1904 treaty adding its lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boun-oum">Prince Boun Oum of Champasak</a> renounced his dynastic claim in 1946 so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> could be unified as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-champasak.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Champasak" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: User13126789 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Champasak.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1713 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Champasak</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Soysisamouth, Boun Oum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lao</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Laos, Thailand, Cambodia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Champassak, Principality of Champasak, Bassac</dd></dl></aside></div>
